# Commits normalise to LF; a Windows checkout gets CRLF back.
#
# Both forms were measured, not assumed. The corpus was run on the CRLF working
# copies, then re-run on the LF form of the same code — read out of the index
# with `git cat-file blob`, which is 0 files containing CRLF and all 5 .gz
# payloads byte-identical to the working copies:
#
#   CRLF working tree    28/28 (100.0%), runs killed: none, 5/5 9/9 18/18 9/9
#   LF form of the code  28/28 (100.0%), runs killed: none, 5/5 9/9 18/18 9/9
#
# An "LF checkout" is not something you can produce here, which is why the
# second row is worded that way. `git checkout-index` under this file's own
# `* text=auto` wrote 82 of 90 files with CRLF even with `core.autocrlf=false`:
# `text=auto` defers to `core.eol`, and `core.eol` is `native` on Windows.
# Only reading the blobs bypasses the smudge filter.
#
# So line endings are not load-bearing for any fixture, and the platform
# default is safe. Only *.gz is pinned, because those fixtures are gzip
# payloads whose bytes are the thing under test. The earlier version of this
# file also claimed the other byte-sensitive fixtures were "already classified
# as binary" by git — untrue; `git check-attr` and the add warnings both show
# latin1.xml is handled as text. It passes in both forms anyway, which is why
# no rule pins it.
* text=auto
*.gz binary
